//@ expect: accept
//@ reason: a derived-class instance can be passed to a FUNCTION PARAMETER
//@ reason: declared as the base type, and virtual dispatch through that
//@ reason: parameter still resolves to the derived override. This is the
//@ reason: base-typed-LOCAL case (`Animal a = new Dog(3); a.speak();`,
//@ reason: already covered by inherit_virtual_dispatch_accepted.clg) extended
//@ reason: to a base-typed PARAMETER -- confirmed via probe to be a genuinely
//@ reason: different code path (AstEmitter::emitMovedArg's std::move handling
//@ reason: for a call argument, not factoryFor's declared-vs-constructed
//@ reason: split), and load-bearing for any future feature that passes a
//@ reason: polymorphic value through a function boundary (e.g. closures
//@ reason: implementing a shared interface).
//@ run: derived 15
//
// Landing this surfaced and fixed a real, general, pre-existing bug (unrelated
// to parameter-passing itself): any class used as a base, if it ALSO declared
// its own explicit zero-arg constructor, got a colliding SECOND `T() =
// default;` synthesized into it by AstEmitter's base-class injection block --
// a real g++ error ("cannot be overloaded with"). Fixed by
// AstEmitter::hasUserDefinedDefaultConstructor, which the injection now
// checks before synthesizing one. `Drawer`'s own explicit `Drawer() { }`
// below is what exercises this.

class Drawer {
public:
    Drawer() { }
    virtual void call(int x) {
        std::cout << "base" << std::endl;
    }
};

class MyDrawer : public Drawer {
public:
    int captured;
    MyDrawer(int c) { captured = c; }
    void call(int x) override {
        std::cout << "derived " << (captured + x) << std::endl;
    }
};

void invoke(Drawer d, int arg) {
    d.call(arg);
}

int main() {
    MyDrawer m = new MyDrawer(10);
    invoke(m, 5);
    return 0;
}
